      "output": "## Cars and boats: eight GLB files, handed on unchanged\n\nAll eight files were read by one standard-library Python script (`inspect_glb.py`) and published byte for byte. Each publish receipt's SHA-256 equals the one the script computed and the one supplied with the upload.\n\n| File | Bytes | Meshes | Triangles | Materials | Textures (embedded WebP: colour + ORM + normal) | Size in metres, X × Y × Z | SHA-256 (first 12) | Same bytes |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|---|---|---|\n| ranchero-v2.glb | 830,340 | 1 | 14,605 | 1 | 3 at 2048×2048: 290,480 + 185,804 + 88,046 B | 0.998 × 0.377 × 0.424 | `ce2118c6f8d0` | same bytes: yes |\n| corvina-spyder.glb | 728,124 | 1 | 11,638 | 1 | 3 at 2048×2048: 250,306 + 210,866 + 60,176 B | 0.998 × 0.295 × 0.412 | `3a0d990a6c05` | same bytes: yes |\n| albatros-gt.glb | 580,324 | 1 | 10,595 | 1 | 3 at 2048×2048: 181,906 + 130,332 + 53,354 B | 0.998 × 0.295 × 0.443 | `35b2f6495d70` | same bytes: yes |\n| sedan.glb | 341,644 | 1 | 6,132 | 1 | 3 at 1024×1024: 86,320 + 95,950 + 43,580 B | 0.998 × 0.334 × 0.432 | `9ee4217d4c0b` | same bytes: yes |\n| suv.glb | 299,916 | 1 | 5,757 | 1 | 3 at 1024×1024: 66,130 + 72,666 + 30,148 B | 0.998 × 0.453 × 0.447 | `ce8c0099a262` | same bytes: yes |\n| boat-yacht.glb | 149,680 | 1 | 3,677 | 1 | 3 at 512×512: 25,458 + 15,664 + 11,720 B | 0.295 × 0.381 × 0.998 | `a06df5f6fd01` | same bytes: yes |\n| boat-fishing.glb | 137,484 | 1 | 3,533 | 1 | 3 at 512×512: 26,840 + 7,700 + 6,964 B | 0.996 × 0.447 × 0.408 | `1101155cbf49` | same bytes: yes |\n| barrier-road.glb | 127,264 | 1 | 1,782 | 1 | 3 at 512×512: 27,490 + 39,324 + 17,924 B | 0.416 × 0.736 × 0.998 | `3b64c94ddd1f` | same bytes: yes |\n\n**How the figures were measured**\n\n- **Structure:** every file is glTF 2.0 with one JSON chunk and one BIN chunk, and the header length equals the file length. Each has one node, one mesh, one indexed triangle primitive and one opaque, double-sided material. There are no animations, skins or external buffers.\n- **Triangles:** index accessor count ÷ 3, read from the JSON.\n- **Size:** POSITION min/max taken through the node's scale (0.499; 0.498 for the fishing boat). Positions are normalised 16-bit integers, so the script divided them by 32767 first.\n- **Textures:** type and pixel size were read from each embedded image's WebP header.\n- **Compression:** all eight list `EXT_meshopt_compression`, `EXT_texture_webp` and `KHR_mesh_quantization` under both `extensionsUsed` and `extensionsRequired`. None uses Draco.\n- **Not done:** I did not decode the meshopt-compressed geometry or render the models, so I have not seen what they look like.\n\n**For the builders:**\n\n- **Loader:** all eight files require a meshopt decoder and WebP texture support; a loader without them will refuse the files.\n- **Scale and origin:** no model is at real-world scale. Each is fitted to about 1 m on its longest side and centred on the origin, so the game must scale each one and lift it onto the road or the water.\n- **Long axis:** it is X for the four cars, the Ranchero and the fishing boat, and Z for the yacht and the barrier. Check headings.\n- **Single mesh:** wheels are not separate parts and cannot spin on their own. The material is opaque, so there is no see-through glass.\n- **Weight:** the Ranchero is the heaviest at 830,340 bytes, about 6.5 times the barrier, and 564,330 of those bytes are its three 2048×2048 textures. The eight files total 3,194,776 bytes.\n- **Textures:** no model is without them.",
